{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Ciosek2000-NCNBPIF-m7-HLH-LHL",
  "Source": "J. Ciosek, \"Narrow-band interference filters with unconventional spacer layers,\" Appl. Opt. 39, 135-140 (2000). doi:10.1364/AO.39.000135 - Fig. 7 curve 4 and Fig. 8 (p.138-139)",
  "Notes": "The same NCNBPIF with the two mirrors in the opposite order\nSpecification: Narrow transmittance peak at 652.22 nm.\nReported: Fig. 8 labels curves 3 and 4 together: same R/T as Ciosek2000-NCNBPIF-m7-LHL-HLH, T = 1 at 652.2 nm, R < 0.5 roughly from 651.5 to 653.0 nm (approximate).\nNotes: Paper notation: 632.8[(0.5H L 0.5H)^7(0.5L H 0.5L)^7] (30 layers after merging). H = quarter wave of n = 2.25, L = quarter wave of n = 1.45 at reference_nm, no dispersion; n_S = n_0 = 1 (Table 1 footnote, Sec. 3), so incident and substrate are both air. Adjacent layers of the same material in the paper's symbol string are merged into one layer (e.g. ...0.5L)(0.5L... becomes one L), which reproduces the paper's own layer counts (27 in Fig. 3, 30 in Fig. 4). Layers are listed in the order the paper writes the symbol string; the paper's Eq. (4) 'S | ... | P' suggests the string starts at the substrate, but because both outer media are n = 1 and the stack is lossless, R and T do not depend on which side faces the light (only the reflection phase does). Normal incidence. This is exactly the reversed stack of Ciosek2000-NCNBPIF-m7-LHL-HLH; with n0 = nS = 1 and no loss its R and T are identical.\nLayers from Fig. 7 curve 4 and Fig. 8 (p.138-139); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T peak 100.000 % at 652.218 nm; paper T = 1 at 652.2 nm.",
  "Wavelength": [
    632.8
  ],
